title The gate asked at the door
prompt The fleet baton, and my own card handoff -- a pre-commit dialect read over staged shell would close shell_dialect's 43-minute detection gap.
rota lap 4256, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; law and boundary, and its sense is touch. The threshold page asks where each thing ends and says a claim of a boundary is tested by pressing on it. This lap pressed the dialect boundary and the hand went through: the fence stands at the roster, half an hour downstream of the lap that climbs it. TAME Core's check at the edge and Single-Stranded's pull-one-part-out test both landed on the same design -- lift the patterns from the elder rather than respell them, so one rule stays in one file.
think Cold open: 184 guards, 179 green, 2 red, 3 gated. Both reds are one -- stash_record at unlanded=3, and standing_equipment reading it. The card names stash_record another seat's (%592), so I left it and took my own named next.
think The elder shell_dialect reading is correct and slow. It stands at tier lap inside a roster pass of roughly half an hour, so the lap that writes a GNU-only idiom is structurally never the lap that hears it. On 20260908 two sed in-place plants landed at 01:59 and were first heard at another ship's cold open at 02:42.
think The design question was what may honestly be gated on a staged blob rather than a diff hunk. Answer, and it is a fact rather than a preference: five of the six families stand at a ceiling of ZERO, so no lawful site exists anywhere in the tree, so every hit in a staged file is a rise and no author is ever refused for a line somebody else wrote.
think That fact is READ rather than asserted. The scan lifts all six patterns, both read-past paths and all six ceilings out of shell_dialect_scan.sh by name and evaluates them, and gates only the families whose ceiling still reads zero. A family whose ceiling later rises goes quiet by itself rather than on the strength of a sentence in a header.
think Proved it against the commit that actually broke it. Cloned the tree to a throwaway, checked out 0f48f2c85, ran the reading in head mode: staged_gated_sites=2, both sites named with file and line. That is the refusal a hand would have met at 01:59.
think One trap met in the building, and it is the elder's own lesson one layer down. The pen's plants and the scan's prove-red line live in files ON the elder's roster, so spelling a gated flag beside its command would have reddened the elder the moment they were tracked. Each is assembled from a flag held in a variable now, and the elder reads zero with all four files staged.
obs The 43 minutes was never the whole cost. Two hands cured the same two sites in parallel and one cure withdrew whole -- a latency long enough for a second ship to start the same repair is a latency that spends two laps on one fault. The wall makes that class impossible rather than cheaper.
obs A whole-blob reading is usually the loose choice and here it is the tight one, purely because the ceiling is zero. I wrote the reason into the scan's header and into the roster row rather than leaving it to be re-derived, since the next hand to widen this to a family standing above zero needs to meet the argument before they do it.
obs The wall is driven, not merely the reading. Four of the 28 pen cases copy tools/hooks/pre-commit into a throwaway repository's hooks directory and run real git commit calls through it -- one refused, one freed by the portable spelling, one staging no shell at all. A scan that refuses says little about a commit somebody actually makes.
obs stash_record stays open and is not mine. unlanded=3 means three session logs stand in round-open stashes with nothing else carrying them; the card assigns it and I left it whole.
loom roster=standing_equipment_run tier=lap guards_run=184 guards_green=179 guards_red=2 guards_gated=3 guards_seconds=1370
